Q: Why are these random numbers printing in the terminal? Everything else seems to be working fine A: <p>Its a common beginner mistake to mix logic with in/output and/or to confuse what a function returns with what a function prints on the terminal. A function should do one thing.
Your function <code>is_palindrome</code> is declared to return a <code>bool</code>. It does determine whether the string is a palindrome, and it prints some text. But then it misses to return a value, hence calling the function from <code>main</code> and attempting to print its return value invokes undefined behavior. The output of the program could be anything.</p>
<p>Let's get this straight. You want to determine whether the string is a palindrome and print text depending on the outcome. For the first, you write a function that does that but nothing else:</p>
<pre><code>bool is_palindrome(const std::string&amp; text){
  std::string rev = text;
  std::reverse(rev.begin(), rev.end());
  return rev == text;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then you can write a function that uses the first and adds output:</p>
<pre><code>void print_palindrome(const std::string&amp; text){
   if (is_palindrome(text)) {
       std::cout &lt;&lt; text &lt;&lt; &quot; is a palindrome!\n&quot;;
   } else {
       std::cout &lt;&lt; text &lt;&lt; &quot; is not a palindrome.\n&quot;;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In <code>main</code> you don't have to print something again:</p>
<pre><code>int main() {
    print_palindrome(&quot;madam&quot;)
    print_palindrome(&quot;fish&quot;)
}
</code></pre>
